Experiment Name: Effects of Different pH Gradients on the Morphology of Extracted Nucleic Acids

Abstract : Using materials found at home, the DNA in the banana was examined macroscopically. Then, the obtained DNA was mixed separately with acids and bases, and the macroscopic appearance of the resulting DNA was examined.

Materials: 
100 ml water
1 banana
15 ml dish soap
1 teaspoon (10-15 gr) salt
1.5 teaspoons baking soda
A little lemon juice
A sealed bag
Simple preparation for 4-layer cheesecloth
2 plates (or 2 different plates with different acidity levels that we can observe separately)
Beaker
80°C alcoholic cologne (should not be cold)

Experiment Description:

First, we begin the experiment by preparing the lysis solution. We add 100 ml of water, 15 ml of dish soap, and 10-15 grams of salt to the lysis solution, and then, using a pipette, we stir the mixture slowly to prevent foaming. (There was slight foaming, and I stirred continuously for 1 minute.)
Once I was sure the solution was ready, I placed a banana in a resealable bag, sealed it tightly to minimize airflow, and began mashing the banana. I continued mashing until I achieved a homogeneous consistency in the banana's texture(I mashed the banana for about 5 minutes).

After mashing the banana, I slowly added the resulting lysis solution to a resealable bag. Then, I stirred the bag for 2.5 minutes to thoroughly homogenize the mixture. Some foaming occurred during this process because the lysis solution contained detergent. After 2.5 minutes, I poured the homogenized mixture into an empty beaker using a four-ply cheesecloth and allowed it to filter. Due to laboratory conditions, I had to wait an hour without moving the filter at all.

An hour later, I removed the filter and added some cologne (containing 80% alcohol) to the liquid we obtained, pouring the cologne from the edge of the glass (the cologne was at room temperature). Pouring it from the edge is very important because it prevents the alcohol and the filtered mixture from mixing.
After adding the cologne, while waiting for the DNA to rise to the surface, I began preparing the acids and bases to be used in the experiment. I chose to use 1.5 teaspoons of baking soda and 1 tablespoon of warm water as the base, and a little more than 1 tablespoon of lemon juice as the acid.

I waited about 15 minutes for the DNA to rise to the top, but since the DNA was held in the middle of the two mixtures (most likely because we were using room temperature cologne, not cold cologne) and the key point in our experiment was to observe the DNA macroscopically and to observe the changes at different pH levels, I placed the DNA into two separate plates using a teaspoon (I added 2 teaspoons of DNA to each plate). Finally, I waited for the solution to separate, and as a result, the DNA added to the carbonated water was softer than the DNA added to the lemon juice, which allowed for macroscopic observation. I have also included videos of the results of this experiment in the file.